    "q-161876": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161876",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{3}+v\\end{aligned}]?\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 1-3{u}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(1-3{u}^{2}) \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,2)} & ={e}^{u+v}(1-3{u}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-2\\,v=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2+2}(1-3(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-11|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =11\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
